'''amun''' (am′un), ''n.'' [Hind. and Beng. ''āman'', ''āmun'', winter rice, sown in July and August, and reaped in December.] Same as ''aghanee.''

'''amurcosity†''' (am-ėr-kos′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''amurcous'': see ''-osity''.] The quality of being amurcous. ''Ash.''

'''amurcous†''' (a-mėr′kus), ''a.'' [< L. ''amurca'', another form of ''amurga'', < Gr. ἀμόργη, usually ἀμόργης, the watery part that runs out when olives are pressed, < ἀμέργειν, press, squeeze, prob. akin to ἀμέλγειν, milk, = E. ''milk'', q. v.] Full of dregs or lees; foul. ''Ash.''

'''amusable''' (a̤-mū′za̤-bl), ''a.'' [< F. ''amusable'': see ''amuse'' and ''-able''.] Capable of being amused.
{{smaller block|Trying to amuse a man who was not ''amusable''.
{{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Sylvia’s Lovers, v.}}
He was otherwise not a very ''amusable'' person, and off his own ground he was not conversable.
{{right|''Howells'', A Modern Instance, xxii.}}}}

'''amuse''' (a̤-mūz′), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''amused'', ppr. ''amusing''. [Early mod. E. also ''amuze'', ''ammuse'', ''ammuze''; < F. ''amuser'', < ''a-'' (L. ''ad'', to) + OF. ''muser'', stare, gaze fixedly, > E. ''muse''<sup>2</sup>, q. v.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To cause to muse; absorb or engage in meditation; occupy or engage wholly; bewilder; puzzle.
{{smaller block|People stood ''amused'' between these two forms of service.
{{right|''Fuller.''}}
''Amuse'' not thyself about the riddles of future things.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Christ. Mor., iii. 13.}}}}

'''2.''' To keep in expectation, as by flattery, plausible pretenses, and the like; delude; keep in play.
{{smaller block|He ''amused'' his followers with idle promises. {{right|''Johnson.''}}
Bishop Henry . . . ''amused'' her with dubious answers, and kept her in suspense for some days.}}

readings.c1s4:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Swift'', King Stephen.}}}}

'''3.''' To fix the attention of agreeably; engage the fancy of; cause to feel cheerful or merry; entertain; divert: as, to ''amuse'' an audience with anecdotes or tricks, or children with toys.
{{smaller block|A group of mountaineer children ''amusing'' themselves with pushing stones from the top.
{{right|''W. Gilpin'', Tour of the Lakes.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|It would be ''amusing'' to make a digest of the irrational laws which bad critics have framed for the government of poets.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Moore’s Byron.}}}}

{{smaller block|While the nation groaned under oppression . . . [Temple] ''amused'' himself by writing memoirs and tying up apricots.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Sir William Temple.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn. 3.''' ''Amuse, Divert, Entertain, Beguile'', occupy, please, enliven. ''Amuse'' may imply merely the prevention of the tedium of idleness or emptiness of mind: as, I can ''amuse'' myself by looking out at the window; or it may suggest a stronger interest: as, I was greatly ''amused'' by their tricks. ''Divert'' is to turn the attention aside, and (in the use considered here) to something light or mirthful. ''Entertain'' is to engage and sustain the attention by something of a pleasing and perhaps instructive character, as conversation; hence the general name ''entertainment'' for lectures, exhibitions, etc., designed to interest in this way. “Whatever ''amuses'' serves to kill time, to lull the faculties and banish reflection; it may be solitary, sedentary, and lifeless: whatever ''diverts'' causes mirth and provokes laughter; it will be active, lively, and tumultuous: whatever ''entertains'' acts on the senses and awakens the understanding; it must be rational and is mostly social.” ''Crabb.'' ''Beguile'' is, figuratively, to cheat one out of weariness, of dull time, etc. The word is as often thus applied to the thing as to the person: as, to ''beguile'' a weary hour; to ''beguile'' one of his cares.}}

{{smaller block|I am careful . . . to ''amuse'' you by the account of all I see.
{{right|''Lady Montagu'', Letters, I. 110. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>The stage its ancient fury thus let fall,
And comedy ''diverted'' without gall.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Art of Poetry, iii. 777.}}}}

{{smaller block|There is so much virtue in eight volumes of Spectators . . . that they are not improper to lie in parlours or summer-houses, to ''entertain'' our thoughts in any moments of leisure.
{{right|''Watts.''}}}}

{{smaller block|The reason of idleness and of crime is the deferring of our hopes. Whilst we are waiting, we ''beguile'' the time with jokes, with sleep, with eating, and with crimes.
{{right|''Emerson'', Nominalist and Realist.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''II.†''' ''intrans.'' To muse; meditate.
{{smaller block|Or in some pathless wilderness ''amusing'',<br>Plucking the mossy bark of some old tree.
{{right|''Lee'', Lucius Junius Brutus.}}}}

'''amusee''' (ạ-mū-zē′), ''n.'' [< ''amuse'' + ''-ee''<sup>1</sup>.] The person amused. ''Carlyle.'' [Rare.]

'''amusement''' (ạ-mūz′ment), ''n.'' [< F. ''amusement'': see ''amuse'' and ''-ment.''] '''1†.''' Absorbing thought; meditation; musing; reverie.
{{smaller block|Here I . . . fell into a strong and deep ''amusement'', revolving in my mind, with great perplexity, the amazing change of our affairs. ''Bp. Fleetwood'', Pref. to Lay Baptism.}}
'''2.''' The state of being amused; mental enjoyment or diversion; moderate mirth or merriment due to an external cause.
{{smaller block|Among the means towards a higher civilization, I unhesitatingly assert that the deliberate cultivation of public ''amusement'' is a principal one.
{{right|''Jevons'', Social Reform, p. 7.}}}}
'''3.''' That which amuses, detains, or engages the mind; pastime; entertainment: as, to provide children with ''amusements''.
{{smaller block|During his confinement, his ''amusement'' was to give poison to cats and dogs, and see them expire by slower or quicker torments. {{right|''Pope.''}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 3.''' ''Entertainment, Diversion'', etc. (see ''pastime''), sport.}}

'''amuser''' (ạ-mū′zėr), ''n.'' One who amuses; one who provides diversion.

'''amusette''' (am-ụ̄-zet′), ''n.'' [F. (dim. of *''amuse''), a light gun, a toy, amusement, < ''amuser'', amuse.] A light field-cannon, invented by Marshal Saxe, designed for outpost service. ''Larousse.''

'''amusingly''' (ạ-mū′zing-li), ''adv.'' In an amusing manner.

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''amusive''' (a̤-mū′ziv), ''a.'' [< ''amuse'' + ''-ive.''] Having power to amuse or entertain the mind; affording amusement or entertainment. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>A grave proficient in ''amusive'' feats
Of puppetry.</poem>{{right|''Wordsworth'', Excursion, v.}}}}

'''amusively''' (a̤-mū′ziv-li), ''adv.'' In an amusive manner.

'''amusiveness''' (a̤-mū′ziv-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being amusive, or of being fitted to afford amusement.

'''amy†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''amy'', ''ami'', < OF. (and mod. F.) ''ami'', m., ''amie'', f., < L. ''amicus'', m., ''amica'', f., a friend: see ''amiable'', ''amor''. So the fem. personal name ''Amy'', which is, however, partly < F. ''Aimée'', < L. ''amata'', fem. of ''amatus'', beloved, pp. of ''amare'', love.] A friend.
{{smaller block|“Thou bel ''amy'', thou pardoner,” he seyde.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Pardoner’s Tale, l. 32.}}}}

'''Amyclæan''' (am-i-klē′a̤n), ''a.'' [< L. ''Amyclæus'', Gr. Ἀμυκλαῖος, < L. ''Amyclæ'', Gr. Ἀμύκλαι.] Of or pertaining to Amyclæ, an ancient town of Laconia, Greece, or to a town of the same name in Latium, or to the inhabitants of either. {{smaller|According to one tradition the inhabitants of the former city, or according to another those of the latter, were so frequently alarmed by false rumors of invasion that a law was made prohibiting all mention of the subject. The result was that when the invasion came no alarm was given, and the city was taken; hence the phrase ''Amyclæan silence.''—'''Amyclæan brothers''', Castor and Pollux, twin sons of Zeus and Leda, born, according to one form of the legend, at Amyclæ in Laconia.}}

